CAT = "bmControl"; SEG = "CTL"
Q = [
('Braking hard on a dry road, which wheel carries most of the load?',
 'The front wheel', ['The rear wheel', 'Both equally', 'Neither, it shifts to the rider'],
 'Braking shifts weight forward onto the front tyre, which is why the front brake does most of the stopping. The rear locks more easily.'),
('You must stop quickly on a bike without ABS. How do you brake?',
 'Both brakes, front squeezed firmly', ['Rear brake only, as hard as possible', 'Front brake grabbed in one go', 'Clutch in and coast to a stop'],
 'Use both brakes, squeezing the front progressively so the load moves onto the front tyre before you brake hardest. Grabbing it can lock the wheel.'),
('Your bike has ABS and you brake hard on a dry road. What does ABS keep for you?',
 'Steering control', ['A shorter stop on gravel', 'Grip in a full lean', 'Braking without the lever'],
 'ABS stops the wheels locking, so you can still steer while braking hard. It does not add grip or replace good technique.'),
('Braking for a sudden queue on a bike without ABS, you feel the front tyre begin to slide. What brings steering back?',
 'Releasing the front brake', ['Holding the lever tighter', 'Pulling in the clutch', 'Pressing the rear brake harder'],
 'A locked front wheel cannot steer and the bike will fall. Release the lever at once so the wheel turns, then reapply more gently.'),
('A tight hairpin waits at the end of a straight. Why do your braking on the straight?',
 'Upright tyres grip better', ['The bike is lighter then', 'The engine is quieter', 'It saves fuel'],
 'Tyres have the most grip for braking when the bike is upright. Brake before the bend, then roll through it with a steady throttle.'),
('Halfway round a bend you must stop for an obstacle. What is the safer way?',
 'Straighten up, then brake firmly', ['Brake hard while still leaning', 'Close the throttle and lean more', 'Use only the front brake'],
 'Braking hard while leaned over can make the tyres slide. Bring the bike more upright first, then brake firmly in a straight line.'),
('At 70 km/h you must swerve left round a pothole. Which grip do you push forward?',
 'The left grip', ['The right grip', 'Both grips together', 'Neither, you lean only'],
 'This is countersteering: pushing the left grip forward leans the bike to the left and turns it left, quickly and precisely.'),
('On a winding road near Durbuy, where should your eyes be in each bend?',
 'On the exit of the bend', ['On your front wheel', 'On the white line beside you', 'On the verge inside the bend'],
 'Looking through the bend to where you want to go helps you judge the line and speed. The bike tends to follow your eyes.'),
('A pothole lies ahead. Why look at the gap beside it, not at the hole?',
 'The bike goes where you look', ['The hole is easier to see later', 'It keeps your visor clear', 'It makes the engine quieter'],
 'Riders tend to steer towards what they stare at. Fix your eyes on the escape route and the bike follows it.'),
('A sharp bend appears ahead on a country road. At what point should your speed be right?',
 'Before you start to lean', ['Once you are leaned over', 'At the exit of the bend', 'Only if traffic comes'],
 "Set your speed while still upright and in a straight line. Changing speed sharply in the lean upsets the bike's balance."),
('Mid-bend on the N63, you snap the throttle shut. What happens to the bike?',
 'Weight lurches onto the front', ['It stands up straight', 'The rear tyre grips more', 'Nothing changes'],
 'Shutting the throttle in a bend throws weight forward and upsets the bike. Keep it steady or open it gently through the bend.'),
('On a long, steep descent in the Ardennes, how do you keep your speed in check?',
 'Use a lower gear', ['Ride in neutral', 'Hold the clutch in', 'Brake hard at the bottom'],
 'A lower gear uses engine braking so you need less brake. Coasting in neutral or with the clutch in takes that away.'),
('Pulling away uphill at lights in Namur, which brake should hold the bike?',
 'The rear brake', ['The front brake only', 'No brake, just revs', 'Both feet on the ground'],
 'Holding the bike on the rear brake leaves your right hand free for the throttle as you let the clutch bite, then you release the brake.'),
('Doing a slow U-turn on a narrow street, what gives you the most control?',
 'Rear brake and clutch control', ['A firm squeeze of the front brake', 'High revs in second gear', 'Both feet down on the road'],
 "At walking pace, the clutch's biting point and a light rear brake keep the bike steady. Grabbing the front brake on full lock can drop it."),
('What is the lifesaver look?',
 'A last look over the shoulder', ['A glance in the left mirror', 'A look at the speedometer', 'A check of the fuel gauge'],
 'Just before you change position or turn, a quick look over the shoulder covers the blind spot that your mirrors miss.'),
('You want to change lanes on the ring road. Why look over your shoulder as well?',
 'Mirrors leave a blind spot', ['It is only needed at night', 'It shows your speed', 'It warns drivers behind'],
 'A car can sit just behind and beside you where no mirror shows it. A shoulder check makes sure the space is clear.'),
('With your partner on the pillion, how does your stopping distance compare with riding solo?',
 'It gets longer', ['It gets shorter', 'It stays the same', 'It halves on wet roads'],
 'The extra weight takes more braking to stop. Leave a bigger gap and brake earlier and more smoothly.'),
('It starts to rain. How should your gap to the vehicle ahead change?',
 'At least double it', ['Keep the same gap', 'Halve it to see better', 'Close up to its tyre tracks'],
 'Braking distances grow on wet roads. Leave at least twice your dry gap so you have time to stop.'),
('A speed wobble starts as you ride over a ridge on the motorway. What is the right reaction?',
 'Ease off, light grip', ['Brake hard at once', 'Open the throttle fully', 'Grip the bars rigidly'],
 'Close the throttle smoothly, keep a light grip and let the bike slow. Braking hard or fighting the bars can make the wobble worse.'),
('You plan to overtake a slow van. When should you change down?',
 'Before you pull out', ['Halfway past the van', 'After you pass it', 'Only if it speeds up'],
 'Select a gear with strong pull before you pull out, so the overtake is short and you are not changing gear beside the van.'),
('You cannot avoid riding over a raised edge in the road. How should you take it?',
 'Upright, with a light grip', ['Leaning hard into it', 'Braking hard right on top of it', 'With the clutch pulled in'],
 'Keep the bike upright and roll over it with a relaxed grip and slight weight on the footrests, then continue. Braking or leaning on it can unsettle the bike.'),
('In fast traffic, why tap the brakes before slowing down?',
 'To warn the driver behind', ['To dry out the brake discs', 'To test the ABS', 'To cool the engine'],
 'A flash of the brake light gives the driver behind early warning that you are about to slow, before you actually brake.'),
('Your rear tyre goes flat on the Brussels ring road. How do you stop?',
 'Slow gently, then pull over', ['Brake hard at the rear', 'Brake hard at the front', 'Swerve onto the verge at once'],
 'Keep a firm grip, close the throttle gradually and let the bike slow. Hard braking on a deflating tyre can make you lose control.'),
('Why keep both feet on the footrests when riding slowly in traffic?',
 'For balance and braking', ['To save fuel', 'To keep the engine cool', 'To make you look calm'],
 'Feet on the footrests keep the bike balanced and your right foot ready on the rear brake. Paddling along with your feet down unsettles it.'),
]
